values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i071$il$, $il$Score the company BEFORE sourcing its leads, then spend the day's 20 from the top$il$, $il$Research agent$il$, $il$Both$il$, $il$blocker$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-15T13:38:22.000Z'::timestamptz, 0, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${"Brad": "2026-09-20T14:40:43.685Z"}$il$::jsonb,
$il$LinkedIn cut Oliver off at 20 connection requests today, with You're out of invitations for now. Whether 20 is permanent is i072. Either way it is far below the 30 a day the counter is set to and nowhere near the 80 Oliver assumed, and it changes what the bottleneck is.

Until now the scarce thing was leads. From today the scarce thing is the 20. A hundred requests a week, and at roughly one in three accepting, about 33 conversations a week. A request spent on a company that would have scored badly is a conversation that does not happen, and it cannot be bought back.

AGREED WITH BRAD, 15 SEPTEMBER: the order inverts. A company is RESEARCHED AND SCORED FIRST, and only then are leads sourced for it. Sourcing a lead at an unscored company is how a scarce request ends up spent on a low value target.

WHAT IS NEEDED:
1. Sourcing gated on score. No lead sourcing for a company that has not been researched and scored. Not a warning, a gate.
2. A Companies view sorted by score, highest first, so it is obvious where the next leads and the next 20 requests should go.
3. The counter corrected to 20 a day and enforced rather than advisory, with today's remaining count visible.
4. Sourcing and sending both driven from that ranked list, not from whichever board is open.

Oliver's words: finding the leads is fine, but having the company priority and the research done first, which is what allows us to score them, is much more important.$il$,
$il$THIS RULE ALREADY EXISTS AND IS NOT BEING FOLLOWED, which is the more useful framing for Brad. The engine's operating model already says, in strict terms: add company, light triage, deep research with the IPID test and the checkout walk, dedup and freeze check, and ONLY THEN source leads. Never hand over an unresearched company. So this is not new logic to invent, it is an existing rule that Lovable does not enforce. That matters because a rule living only in prose gets skipped, and the fix is to make the system refuse rather than to remember harder.

THE CURRENT QUEUE IS THE EVIDENCE. The Lovable Master List holds 130 leads added on 9 September, all showing no activity. Brad flagged the same thing on the call: there are contacts sitting against companies that have not been researched, and drafts cannot be built for them. Those 130 were sourced ahead of the research, which is exactly the order this item reverses.

SO THERE IS AN ACTION BEFORE ANY BUILD: score the companies behind those 130 before spending another day's requests on them. Otherwise the first 20 tomorrow go out in the old order regardless of what gets built later.

IT PUTS A DAILY PRICE ON i001. The score needs research, research needs the agent, the agent needs the Supabase connector Oliver still cannot log into. The login failure is no longer just delaying a feature, it is the reason 20 requests a day go out against an unranked list. That is the argument for getting the screenshare done rather than waiting.

ON AUTOMATICALLY SENDING THE REQUESTS, the one part to be careful with. Queueing them in score order, yes. Firing them from the tool is what i019 parks as an account risk, and Oliver has just BEEN throttled, so the account is already under scrutiny. Losing Sales Navigator would cost more than every hour of clicking this saves. Suggested split: the system decides who and in what order and puts the profile one click away, a human sends. Revisit once out of throttle.

A RANKED LIST IS ONLY AS GOOD AS ITS DENOMINATOR. Unassessed is removed from the scoring denominator, never scored zero, so a thinly researched company can score high on three components of four and outrank a fully assessed one. Show confidence next to the score or the 20 a day get spent on whichever companies are least researched. Same trap as i039.

WHAT ELSE THE RANKED VIEW SHOULD CARRY: how many decision makers are sourced, their CONNECTION DEGREE, whether a request is already pending there, the country, and whether the company is frozen. Degree matters more than it looks: second degree accepts materially better than third, the allowance keys off acceptance, so sorting by score AND degree protects the budget. A good share of the current 130 are third degree.

THE FLOOR STILL APPLIES LOOSELY. Oliver confirmed on 15 September that roughly 1,500 devices a month is fine for Germany and the funnel should not be narrowed further, because scoring handles prioritisation. That holds. The 20 a day does the rationing now, not the floor.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i072$il$, $il$The 20 a day is a throttle, not a ceiling. Work out what caused it$il$, $il$Outreach & drafting$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, $il$question$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Claude$il$, '2026-09-15T13:39:22.000Z'::timestamptz, null, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$LinkedIn's own wording settles most of this. The dialog reads: You're out of invitations FOR NOW. We encourage sending fewer, more thoughtful invitations to people you know to keep LinkedIn a trusted community.

That is behavioural throttling language, not a published cap. For now means it can come back, and the sentence about people you know is LinkedIn saying the acceptance rate is the problem. So before the whole engine is designed around 20 a day, work out what pushed it down.

The likely cause is the pile of unaccepted invitations. In August the account carried 255 pending. A large pending stack with a low acceptance rate is the textbook trigger for exactly this.

If that is it, the fix is not to throttle harder. Withdraw the stale invitations, which is already a defined monthly process, and watch the allowance over the next fortnight.

The ranking work in i071 goes ahead regardless. This only decides how tight the ration has to be, and whether it is permanent.$il$,
$il$HOW TO ANSWER IT, cheaply: count the invitations still pending in Sales Navigator and how many are older than 28 days. The monthly cleanup rule already withdraws anything unaccepted at 28 days in one batch, and a withdrawal resets the three week re-invite clock, so there is no cost beyond the clicking.

WATCH IT AFTER, not immediately. LinkedIn does not restore the allowance instantly. Note the daily cap for two weeks after a cleanup. If it recovers, we know the lever and keeping the pending pile small becomes routine rather than monthly.

THE 130 IN THE LOVABLE MASTER LIST ARE PART OF THE PICTURE. All added 9 September, all showing no activity. That is a queue, and if it goes out as 130 requests to people who mostly do not accept, the throttle gets worse rather than better. Which is the i071 argument: send fewer, better aimed.

DEGREE IS A FREE PREDICTOR AND IT IS ALREADY IN THE DATA. On that same screen Christian Haid, Thomas Fugmann and Sebastian Stangenberg are 3rd degree; Alejandro del Valle and Marc Mumm are 2nd. Second degree means a shared connection, which accepts materially better than a cold third degree. Since the allowance keys off acceptance, sorting the queue by degree as well as by score costs nothing and protects the budget. Worth adding to the ranked view in i071.

IT ALSO MAKES THE BLANK CONNECTION REQUEST A THROUGHPUT DECISION, not only a voice one. Anything that lifts acceptance lifts the allowance, so the existing blank-by-default rule is now worth measuring rather than assuming.$il$);

insert into public.improvements (team_id, item_key, title, category, owner, priority, status, raised_by, created_at, build_wave, blocks, blocked_by, done_by, done_at, pinned, hidden, seen_by, detail, notes)
values ((select id from public.teams limit 1), $il$i073$il$, $il$Cooldown says In cooldown but never says until when$il$, $il$Lovable UI$il$, $il$Brad$il$, $il$must$il$, $il$open$il$, $il$Oliver$il$, '2026-09-16T08:28:33.000Z'::timestamptz, 5, '{}'::text[], '{}'::text[], null, null, false, false, $il${}$il$::jsonb,
$il$On Morten Jensen the Cooldown field reads In cooldown. That is a yes or no, and the useful part is the date. Show when it ends: In cooldown until 2 March 2027, with the months remaining next to it.

The date is not something new to calculate. A withdrawn connection request sets Cooldown Until at six months, and that value already exists in the lake as a date. Lovable is displaying the boolean and dropping the date it came from.

Make it sortable and filterable too, so it is possible to ask what comes free next month rather than finding out by accident.$il$,
$il$THE HEADER TILE IS ALREADY THE RIGHT IDEA. Ready to re-engage, cooldown expired, currently 0. That is the queue this feeds, and it only works if every contact carries a real date. A boolean cannot produce that count correctly, it can only produce it by re-deriving the date somewhere else, which is how two places end up disagreeing.

SHOW THE REASON WITH THE DATE. Six months from a withdrawn request is a different thing from a cooldown set after a not right now reply. One is a rule, the other is something a person or a prospect said. When Oliver decides whether to override, the reason is what he needs, not just the date.

OVERRIDE IS OLIVER ONLY, per the standing rule. A cooldown that anything can clear is not a cooldown. Showing the date makes overriding easier, so the control needs to stay deliberate.

SAME TREATMENT ANYWHERE A STATE HIDES A DATE. Next action, research stage outdated in i029, follow ups in i063. A state without its date tells you that something is true but not whether it is today's problem, which is the only question being asked.$il$);
insert into public.improvement_comments (team_id, improvement_id, who, at, text)
select i.team_id, i.id, $il$Claude$il$, '2026-09-17T13:30:00.000Z'::timestamptz,
$il$17 Sept, a worse version of the same fault. On Marcel de Groot the Cooldown field reads NOT IN COOLDOWN, while the record's own Next Action reads DECIDED: CR WITHDRAWN 2026-08-25 by Oliver, no re-CR before 2027-02-25, and Next Action Date is set to 25 February 2027. So a six month re-invite block is in force and the field that exists to say so reports the opposite.

That is not a missing date, it is a false negative on a guard, and it is the direction that costs something: a contact who looks free to approach and is not. Same pattern on Ben Beasley, see i099, where Cooldown reads Not in cooldown next to a cooldown running to 25 February 2027.

Worth establishing whether the Cooldown field is derived from the withdrawal date at all, or whether it is a separate value that has to be set by hand. If the latter, it will be wrong on every contact withdrawn in the August cleanup, which was roughly 138 records.$il$
